body:
So, here's a guide on how to add a new tool, courtesy of Claude. Hope it helps.

---

# Adding a New Tool to ART

This guide walks through every file you must touch to add a new image-processing
tool to ART, from the parameter definition down to the processing code and the
GUI. Three pieces of ART's architecture make this simpler than it might look, and
the guide leans on all three: a flat `ParamsEdited` (one flag per tool), a single
centralized staged pipeline that lists every processing step in order, and a
runtime event mapper that lets a tool register its own reprocessing events
without touching any shared tables.

Throughout, we use a hypothetical tool called **Foo** with a member name `foo`
and two parameters (`amount`, `mode`) plus the usual `enabled` flag. Wherever you
see `Foo`/`foo`, substitute your tool's name. The two best real tools to read
alongside this guide are **Film Grain** (`rtgui/grain.*`, `rtengine/ipgrain.cc`)
and **CA Correction** (`rtgui/cacorrection.*`), both small and self-contained.

## Overview — the moving parts

A tool touches four layers, in this order:

1. **Parameters** (`rtengine/procparams.*`, `rtgui/paramsedited.*`) — the data
   model: a `FooParams` struct, serialization to/from the `.arp` profile, and a
   partial-paste flag.
2. **Processing** (`rtengine/improcfun.h`, a new `rtengine/ipfoo.cc`,
   `rtengine/improcfun.cc`) — the algorithm and its slot in the pipeline.
3. **Events** (via `rtgui/eventmapper.h`) — how a GUI change tells the engine
   which part of the pipeline to re-run.
4. **GUI** (`rtgui/foo.*`, `rtgui/toolpanelcoord.*`,
   `rtgui/partialpastedlg.cc`) — the tool panel and its registration.
5. **Localization** (`rtdata/languages/default` and friends) — the UI strings.
6. **Build** (`rtengine/CMakeLists.txt`, `rtgui/CMakeLists.txt`) — the two new
   `.cc` files.

A complete checklist is at the end.

---

## 1. Parameters

### 1.1 Declare the struct — `rtengine/procparams.h`

Add a struct next to the existing tool structs. Every tool struct has the same
shape: an `enabled` flag, the data fields, a default constructor, and
`operator==`/`operator!=`. Model it on `CACorrParams`
(`rtengine/procparams.h`):

```cpp
struct CACorrParams {
    bool enabled;
    double red;
    double blue;

    CACorrParams();

    bool operator==(const CACorrParams &other) const;
    bool operator!=(const CACorrParams &other) const;
};
```

Your version:

```cpp
struct FooParams {
    enum class Mode { LINEAR, LOG };

    bool enabled;
    double amount;
    Mode mode;

    FooParams();

    bool operator==(const FooParams &other) const;
    bool operator!=(const FooParams &other) const;
};
```

Then add a member to the `ProcParams` class, in the block of tool members
(around the `gradient`/`cacorrection`/`vignetting` lines):

```cpp
    CACorrParams cacorrection;     ///< Lens c/a correction parameters
    FooParams foo;                 ///< Foo tool parameters
```

> The member name `foo` is the identifier used **everywhere else** — defaults,
> save, load, equality, and the `ParamsEdited` flag must all use the same name,
> because some of the machinery substitutes it textually via macros.

If your tool supports **masks / regions** (like `SmoothingParams` or
`ColorCorrectionParams`), derive from `MaskableParams` instead of a plain struct
and hold `std::vector<Region>` + `std::vector<Mask>`. That is a larger topic;
copy `SmoothingParams` wholesale if you need it. The rest of this guide assumes a
plain, non-mask tool.

### 1.2 Implement the struct — `rtengine/procparams.cc`

Defaults live in the **constructor** (not in `setDefaults`). Add near the other
tool implementations:

```cpp
FooParams::FooParams():
    enabled(false), amount(0.0), mode(Mode::LINEAR)
{
}

bool FooParams::operator==(const FooParams &other) const
{
    return enabled == other.enabled && amount == other.amount &&
           mode == other.mode;
}

bool FooParams::operator!=(const FooParams &other) const
{
    return !(*this == other);
}
```

### 1.3 Reset — `ProcParams::setDefaults()`

`setDefaults()` just re-assigns a fresh default-constructed struct. Add one line:

```cpp
    foo = FooParams();
```

### 1.4 Save to the profile — `ProcParams::save(...)`

Find the big `ProcParams::save(ProgressListener*, bool, KeyFile&, const
ParamsEdited*, ...)` function. Near its top it defines:

```cpp
#define RELEVANT_(n) (!pedited || pedited->n)
```

Add a save block alongside the others (e.g. right after the `CACorrection`
block). Use `saveToKeyfile` for scalars/bools; use the enum overload (which takes
a `std::map<T, const char*>`) for enum fields:

```cpp
        // Foo
        if (RELEVANT_(foo)) {
            saveToKeyfile("Foo", "Enabled", foo.enabled, keyFile);
            saveToKeyfile("Foo", "Amount", foo.amount, keyFile);
            saveToKeyfile("Foo", "Mode", {
                    {FooParams::Mode::LINEAR, "Linear"},
                    {FooParams::Mode::LOG, "Log"}
                }, foo.mode, keyFile);
        }
```

`"Foo"` is the `[Foo]` group name in the `.arp` file; `"Enabled"`, `"Amount"`,
`"Mode"` are the keys. Pick stable names — they become part of the on-disk
format.

### 1.5 Load from the profile — `ProcParams::load(...)`

Find the matching `ProcParams::load(...)`. The load side **always double-guards**
on `has_group(...)` and `RELEVANT_(...)`, and uses `assignFromKeyfile`
(which only assigns when the key is present):

```cpp
        if (keyFile.has_group("Foo") && RELEVANT_(foo)) {
            assignFromKeyfile(keyFile, "Foo", "Enabled", foo.enabled);
            assignFromKeyfile(keyFile, "Foo", "Amount", foo.amount);
            assignFromKeyfile(keyFile, "Foo", "Mode", {
                    {"Linear", FooParams::Mode::LINEAR},
                    {"Log", FooParams::Mode::LOG}
                }, foo.mode);
        }
```

Note the enum map is **inverted** relative to save (string → enum on load).

### 1.6 Equality — `ProcParams::operator==`

Near the bottom of `procparams.cc`, `operator==` ANDs a `ppEQ_` macro over every
tool member. Add your member into the `&&` chain:

```cpp
    ppEQ_(cacorrection) && ppEQ_(foo) && ppEQ_(vignetting) &&
```

Miss this and profile-change detection / the History panel will not notice edits
to your tool.

### 1.7 Partial-paste flag — `rtgui/paramsedited.h` / `.cc`

`ParamsEdited` records, per tool, whether that tool should be affected by a
partial operation (such as pasting only part of a profile). It is a flat class
with **one flag per tool** — there is no per-field granularity — so the
authoritative list of tools is simply its set of boolean members
(`rtgui/paramsedited.h`). Add one:

```cpp
    bool foo;
```

Then in `rtgui/paramsedited.cc`, add an assignment inside `set()`:

```cpp
void ParamsEdited::set(bool v)
{
    ...
    foo = v;
    ...
}
```

That is all a plain tool needs. Only the four tri-state mask tools
(`colorcorrection`, `smoothing`, `localContrast`, `textureBoost`) are `unsigned`
and appear in `set_append()` — ignore that unless your tool is a mask tool.

The connection to save/load: `RELEVANT_(foo)` expands to `!pedited ||
pedited->foo`. When no `pedited` is supplied (normal save/load) everything is
processed; when a partial profile supplies one (partial paste), only tools whose
flag is `true` are touched. The member name in `ProcParams` **must** match the
flag name in `ParamsEdited`.

---

## 2. Processing

### 2.1 Declare the method — `rtengine/improcfun.h`

Every tool is a member method of `ImProcFunctions` operating on an
`Imagefloat *`. There are two conventions:

- `void foo(Imagefloat *rgb)` — always continues the pipeline.
- `bool foo(Imagefloat *rgb)` — returns `true` to **stop** the pipeline (used
  only when the tool paints a mask/preview overlay into the buffer instead of the
  image).

Use `void` unless you need mask-preview. Add the declaration next to the others
(around the `filmGrain`/`logEncoding`/`softLight` block):

```cpp
    void filmGrain(Imagefloat *rgb);
    void foo(Imagefloat *rgb);
```

### 2.2 Implement it — new file `rtengine/ipfoo.cc`

Follow the `rtengine/ipsoftlight.cc` template — the cleanest minimal tool. Four
load-bearing patterns: **(1)** an early `enabled` guard, **(2)** coerce the
shared buffer into the color space you need with `setMode`, **(3)** read all
parameters from the `params` member, **(4)** OpenMP-guarded pixel loops honoring
the `multiThread` member.

```cpp
#ifdef _OPENMP
#include <omp.h>
#endif

#include "improcfun.h"
#include "rt_math.h"

namespace rtengine {

void ImProcFunctions::foo(Imagefloat *rgb)
{
    // (1) enabled guard — always first
    if (!params->foo.enabled || params->foo.amount == 0.0) {
        return;
    }

    // (2) force the buffer into the color space this tool works in.
    //     Use Mode::RGB for RGB tools, Mode::LAB for Lab tools (there g() is L).
    rgb->setMode(Imagefloat::Mode::RGB, multiThread);

    // (3) read parameters exclusively from `params`
    const float amount = params->foo.amount;
    const int W = rgb->getWidth();
    const int H = rgb->getHeight();

    // (4) pixel loop, OpenMP guarded by the multiThread member
#ifdef _OPENMP
#   pragma omp parallel for if (multiThread)
#endif
    for (int y = 0; y < H; ++y) {
        for (int x = 0; x < W; ++x) {
            rgb->r(y, x) *= (1.f + amount);
            rgb->g(y, x) *= (1.f + amount);
            rgb->b(y, x) *= (1.f + amount);
        }
    }
}

} // namespace rtengine
```

Useful members available inside any `ImProcFunctions` method: `scale` (preview
subsampling factor — divide radii/sizes by it so the preview matches the full
image), `offset_x` / `offset_y`, `full_width` / `full_height`, `cur_pipeline`
(`OUTPUT` / `PREVIEW` / `NAVIGATOR`), and `multiThread`. For a heavy algorithm,
keep the public method thin (guard + param reading + orchestration) and put the
number-crunching in a free function in an anonymous namespace, as
`iplocalcontrast.cc` and `ipsharpen.cc` do.

### 2.3 Insert into the pipeline — `rtengine/improcfun.cc`

ART centralizes the **entire tool sequence** in one function,
`ImProcFunctions::process(Pipeline, Stage, Imagefloat*)`. This is the single
place that lists every step in order. Tools are invoked through two macros:

```cpp
#define STEP_(op)   apply<void>(&ImProcFunctions::op, img)   // void tools
#define STEP_s_(op) apply<bool>(&ImProcFunctions::op, img)   // bool (stop) tools
```

The function is a `switch` over four stages. Add your `STEP_` call at the
position you want, in the appropriate stage:

```cpp
    case Stage::STAGE_3:
        STEP_(creativeGradients);
        stop = stop || STEP_s_(textureBoost);
        if (!stop) {
            STEP_(filmGrain);
            STEP_(foo);              // <-- your tool, after grain, before log
            STEP_(logEncoding);
            STEP_(saturationVibrance);
            ...
```

Choosing the stage matters because each stage runs in a specific color space and
at a specific point in the tone/color chain. As a rough guide:

| Stage     | Contains (examples)                              | Typical use |
|-----------|--------------------------------------------------|-------------|
| `STAGE_0` | dehaze, dynamic range compression                | very early, scene-referred |
| `STAGE_1` | channel mixer, exposure, HSL, tone equalizer     | linear RGB, pre-tone |
| `STAGE_2` | sharpening, denoise, defringe, color correction  | detail / local |
| `STAGE_3` | grain, log encoding, saturation, curves, B&W     | creative / display-referred |

Insert your `STEP_` between the neighbours whose "before/after" ordering you
want. For a `bool` (stop) tool, follow the `stop = stop || STEP_s_(foo);`
pattern and place it where the guarding `if (!stop)` logic makes sense.

Optionally bump `NUM_PIPELINE_STEPS` (a constant near the top of the same file)
so the progress bar stays accurate.

You do **not** need to edit `dcrop.cc`, `simpleprocess.cc`, or
`improccoordinator.cc` — all three call `process()` generically, so a tool added
to a stage automatically runs in the interactive preview, the batch/export
output, and the navigator.

---

## 3. Events (making a GUI change re-process)

When the user changes a widget, the panel calls
`listener->panelChanged(EvSomething, description)`. The engine looks up which
part of the pipeline that event affects and re-runs only what is needed. ART
creates these events **at runtime** via the event mapper — you do **not** edit
the static `procevents.h` enum or the `refreshmap.cc` array for a new tool.

In your panel's constructor (see §4) you request events:

```cpp
#include "eventmapper.h"
...
    auto m = ProcEventMapper::getInstance();
    EvEnabled = m->newEvent(rtengine::RGBCURVE, "HISTORY_MSG_FOO_ENABLED");
    EvAmount  = m->newEvent(rtengine::RGBCURVE, "HISTORY_MSG_FOO_AMOUNT");
    EvMode    = m->newEvent(rtengine::RGBCURVE, "HISTORY_MSG_FOO_MODE");
```

The first argument (the "action") is a **refresh level** from
`rtengine/refreshmap.h`. It determines which pipeline stages re-run. The
mapping between refresh masks and stages is:

- `M_RGBCURVE` → re-runs from `STAGE_1`
- `M_LUMACURVE` → re-runs from `STAGE_2`
- `M_LUMINANCE | M_COLOR` → re-runs `STAGE_3` only

Pick the action constant matching the **earliest** stage your tool needs
re-run. Convenient pre-defined constants (all in `refreshmap.h`):

| Constant          | Re-runs from | Use for a tool in |
|-------------------|--------------|-------------------|
| `RGBCURVE`        | STAGE_1      | STAGE_1 / STAGE_2 / STAGE_3 |
| `SHARPENING`      | STAGE_2      | STAGE_2 / STAGE_3 |
| `LUMINANCECURVE`  | STAGE_3      | STAGE_3 |
| `DIRPYREQUALIZER` | STAGE_3      | STAGE_3 |
| `TRANSFORM`       | geometry     | geometry tools |

For our `foo` tool placed in `STAGE_3`, `LUMINANCECURVE` (or the equivalent
`DIRPYREQUALIZER`, which Film Grain uses) is the minimal correct choice;
`RGBCURVE` also works but re-runs more than necessary. The second argument is the
name of a **history-message** translation key (see §5) shown in the History
panel; pass `""` if you don't want a history entry.

Under the hood, `ProcEventMapper::newEvent` calls
`RefreshMapper::getInstance()->newEvent()` for a fresh event id and
`mapEvent(event, action)` to record its refresh level — so the action you pass
here *is* the refresh-map entry. No static table edit is required.

---

## 4. GUI

### 4.1 The panel — new files `rtgui/foo.h` / `rtgui/foo.cc`

Model on `rtgui/grain.*`. The panel inherits `ToolParamBlock` (the widget
container), `FoldableToolPanel` (the collapsible expander + enable checkbox +
reset button + the `read`/`write`/`setDefaults` virtuals), and any listener
interfaces for the widgets it uses (`AdjusterListener` for sliders).

`rtgui/foo.h`:

```cpp
#pragma once

#include "adjuster.h"
#include "toolpanel.h"
#include <gtkmm.h>

class Foo: public ToolParamBlock,
           public AdjusterListener,
           public FoldableToolPanel {
public:
    Foo();

    void read(const rtengine::procparams::ProcParams *pp) override;
    void write(rtengine::procparams::ProcParams *pp) override;
    void setDefaults(const rtengine::procparams::ProcParams *defParams) override;
    void adjusterChanged(Adjuster *a, double newval) override;
    void adjusterAutoToggled(Adjuster *a, bool newval) override {}
    void enabledChanged() override;
    void modeChanged();
    void toolReset(bool to_initial) override;

private:
    Adjuster *amount;
    MyComboBoxText *mode;

    rtengine::ProcEvent EvEnabled;
    rtengine::ProcEvent EvAmount;
    rtengine::ProcEvent EvMode;

    rtengine::procparams::FooParams initial_params;
};
```

`rtgui/foo.cc`:

```cpp
#include "foo.h"
#include "eventmapper.h"

using namespace rtengine;
using namespace rtengine::procparams;

Foo::Foo():
    FoldableToolPanel(this, "foo", M("TP_FOO_LABEL"), false, true, true)
    //                                                 |      |     |
    //                                       need11 ---+      |     |
    //                                       useEnabled (on/off) ---+     |
    //                                       useReset (reset button) ----+
{
    auto m = ProcEventMapper::getInstance();
    EvEnabled = m->newEvent(rtengine::RGBCURVE, "HISTORY_MSG_FOO_ENABLED");
    EvAmount  = m->newEvent(rtengine::RGBCURVE, "HISTORY_MSG_FOO_AMOUNT");
    EvMode    = m->newEvent(rtengine::RGBCURVE, "HISTORY_MSG_FOO_MODE");
    EvToolReset.set_action(rtengine::RGBCURVE);

    amount = Gtk::manage(new Adjuster(M("TP_FOO_AMOUNT"), 0., 100., 1., 0.));
    amount->setAdjusterListener(this);
    amount->show();

    Gtk::HBox *hb = Gtk::manage(new Gtk::HBox());
    hb->pack_start(*Gtk::manage(new Gtk::Label(M("TP_FOO_MODE") + ": ")),
                   Gtk::PACK_SHRINK);
    mode = Gtk::manage(new MyComboBoxText());
    mode->append(M("TP_FOO_MODE_LINEAR"));
    mode->append(M("TP_FOO_MODE_LOG"));
    mode->signal_changed().connect(sigc::mem_fun(*this, &Foo::modeChanged));
    hb->pack_start(*mode);
    hb->show();

    pack_start(*amount);
    pack_start(*hb);
}

void Foo::read(const ProcParams *pp)
{
    disableListener();                // block callbacks while loading
    setEnabled(pp->foo.enabled);
    amount->setValue(pp->foo.amount);
    mode->set_active(int(pp->foo.mode));
    enableListener();
}

void Foo::write(ProcParams *pp)
{
    pp->foo.enabled = getEnabled();
    pp->foo.amount = amount->getValue();
    pp->foo.mode = FooParams::Mode(mode->get_active_row_number());
}

void Foo::setDefaults(const ProcParams *defParams)
{
    amount->setDefault(defParams->foo.amount);
    initial_params = defParams->foo;   // saved for toolReset
}

void Foo::adjusterChanged(Adjuster *a, double newval)
{
    if (listener && getEnabled()) {
        if (a == amount) {
            listener->panelChanged(EvAmount, a->getTextValue());
        }
    }
}

void Foo::modeChanged()
{
    if (listener && getEnabled()) {
        listener->panelChanged(EvMode, mode->get_active_text());
    }
}

void Foo::enabledChanged()
{
    if (listener) {
        if (get_inconsistent()) {
            listener->panelChanged(EvEnabled, M("GENERAL_UNCHANGED"));
        } else if (getEnabled()) {
            listener->panelChanged(EvEnabled, M("GENERAL_ENABLED"));
        } else {
            listener->panelChanged(EvEnabled, M("GENERAL_DISABLED"));
        }
    }
}

void Foo::toolReset(bool to_initial)
{
    ProcParams pp;
    if (to_initial) {
        pp.foo = initial_params;
    }
    pp.foo.enabled = getEnabled();
    read(&pp);
}
```

Key rules:

- `read()` copies params → widgets; wrap it in `disableListener()` /
  `enableListener()` so it doesn't fire change events.
- `write()` copies widgets → params. It must be the exact inverse of `read()`.
- `adjusterChanged` / `modeChanged` fire `listener->panelChanged(EvXxx, text)`,
  guarded by `if (listener && getEnabled())`. `listener` is the coordinator,
  wired up automatically (§4.2).
- `setDefaults` stashes `initial_params` for the reset button; `toolReset`
  restores it.

### 4.2 Register with the coordinator — `rtgui/toolpanelcoord.*`

In `rtgui/toolpanelcoord.h`:

1. Add the include near the other tool includes:
   ```cpp
   #include "foo.h"
   ```
2. Add a member pointer in the `protected` block with the other panels:
   ```cpp
   Foo *foo;
   ```

In `rtgui/toolpanelcoord.cc`, in the constructor:

3. Allocate it with the other `Gtk::manage(new ...)` calls:
   ```cpp
   foo = Gtk::manage(new Foo());
   ```
4. Add it to a tab with `addfavoritePanel(<panel>, foo);`. The tab VBoxes are
   `exposurePanel`, `detailsPanel`, `colorPanel`, `transformPanel`, `rawPanel`,
   `localPanel`, `effectsPanel`. For example, to put Foo on the Effects tab:
   ```cpp
   addfavoritePanel(effectsPanel, foo);
   ```
   To nest it under another tool, pass that tool's `getPackBox()` and a level:
   `addfavoritePanel(lenspanel->getPackBox(), foo, 2);`.

That is the entire registration. `read()`, `write()`, `setDefaults()`, the
editor provider, and the fold-state handling all iterate the internal
`toolPanels` / `expList` vectors, which `addfavoritePanel` → `addPanel`
populate — so there is nothing else to wire. The line
`for (auto toolPanel : toolPanels) toolPanel->setListener(this);` later in the
constructor connects your panel's `listener` to the coordinator automatically.

### 4.3 Partial paste dialog — `rtgui/partialpastedlg.cc`

The dialog is fully table-driven. Add one row to the `get_toggles(...)` table,
in whichever group column fits (the `index` is the group: 0 Exposure, 1 Detail,
2 Effects, 3 Color, 4 Lens, 5 Composition, 6 Local, 7 Raw, 8 Meta). For a plain
`bool` flag, pass its address as the third field and `nullptr` as the last:

```cpp
        {"PARTIALPASTE_FOO", &pedited.foo, 2, nullptr},
```

(Only the four tri-state mask tools use the last `unsigned*` field instead; a
plain tool leaves it `nullptr` and passes `&pedited.foo` as the `bool*`.) The
first field, `"PARTIALPASTE_FOO"`, is a translation key (§5).

---

## 5. Localization

All UI text goes through the `M("KEY")` macro, which looks the key up in the
language files under `rtdata/languages/`. The canonical file is
`rtdata/languages/default` (English); every key you reference must exist there.
Add entries for every string you introduced:

```
HISTORY_MSG_FOO_ENABLED;Foo
HISTORY_MSG_FOO_AMOUNT;Foo - Amount
HISTORY_MSG_FOO_MODE;Foo - Mode
PARTIALPASTE_FOO;Foo
TP_FOO_LABEL;Foo
TP_FOO_AMOUNT;Amount
TP_FOO_MODE;Mode
TP_FOO_MODE_LINEAR;Linear
TP_FOO_MODE_LOG;Log
```

Convention: `TP_*` for tool-panel labels, `HISTORY_MSG_*` for History-panel
descriptions, `PARTIALPASTE_*` for the paste dialog. The format is
`KEY;Text`, one per line, kept sorted. You only need to add to `default`;
translators fill in the other language files, and missing keys fall back to the
`default` text.

---

## 6. Build

Add each new `.cc` (headers are picked up implicitly) to the appropriate
`CMakeLists.txt`:

- `rtgui/CMakeLists.txt` — add `foo.cc` to the `NONCLISOURCEFILES` set.
- `rtengine/CMakeLists.txt` — add `ipfoo.cc` to the engine source list.

Then rebuild. A `.arp` profile written by the new build will carry a `[Foo]`
group; older builds ignore unknown groups, and your `load` code ignores missing
ones, so the format stays forward/backward compatible.

---

## Checklist

Parameters:

- [ ] `rtengine/procparams.h` — `struct FooParams { ... }` + `FooParams foo;` member.
- [ ] `rtengine/procparams.cc` — constructor (defaults), `operator==`, `operator!=`.
- [ ] `rtengine/procparams.cc` — `setDefaults()`: `foo = FooParams();`.
- [ ] `rtengine/procparams.cc` — `save()`: `if (RELEVANT_(foo)) { saveToKeyfile(...); }`.
- [ ] `rtengine/procparams.cc` — `load()`: `if (has_group("Foo") && RELEVANT_(foo)) { assignFromKeyfile(...); }`.
- [ ] `rtengine/procparams.cc` — `operator==`: add `ppEQ_(foo) &&`.
- [ ] `rtgui/paramsedited.h` — `bool foo;`.
- [ ] `rtgui/paramsedited.cc` — `foo = v;` in `set()`.

Processing:

- [ ] `rtengine/improcfun.h` — declare `void foo(Imagefloat *rgb);`.
- [ ] `rtengine/ipfoo.cc` — implement (guard → `setMode` → read params → pixel loop).
- [ ] `rtengine/improcfun.cc` — insert `STEP_(foo);` in the right `Stage`; optionally bump `NUM_PIPELINE_STEPS`.

GUI + events:

- [ ] `rtgui/foo.h` / `rtgui/foo.cc` — the panel (events via `ProcEventMapper`).
- [ ] `rtgui/toolpanelcoord.h` — `#include "foo.h"` + `Foo *foo;`.
- [ ] `rtgui/toolpanelcoord.cc` — allocate + `addfavoritePanel(<tab>, foo);`.
- [ ] `rtgui/partialpastedlg.cc` — one row in `get_toggles`.

Strings + build:

- [ ] `rtdata/languages/default` — `TP_*`, `HISTORY_MSG_*`, `PARTIALPASTE_*` keys.
- [ ] `rtgui/CMakeLists.txt` — `foo.cc`.
- [ ] `rtengine/CMakeLists.txt` — `ipfoo.cc`.

---

## Reference tools to copy from

| Aspect | Read this |
|--------|-----------|
| Minimal params + panel | `CACorrParams` in `procparams.*`, `rtgui/cacorrection.*` |
| Params + panel + enum combo + dynamic events | Film Grain: `rtgui/grain.*`, `GrainParams` |
| Minimal processing method | `rtengine/ipsoftlight.cc` |
| Processing that delegates to another tool | `rtengine/ipgrain.cc` |
| Lab-space, mask-aware, stop-returning tool | `rtengine/iplocalcontrast.cc` |
| Mask / region tool (params) | `SmoothingParams`, `ColorCorrectionParams` |
